# 0003 · Vulnerabilities in the platform image that come from Frappe

- Status: accepted
- Date: @@TODAY@@
- Guide version: @@GUIDE_VERSION@@
- Rules concerned: SEC-VULN-002, SEC-VULN-005

## Context

`just scan-image` fails on every critical or high vulnerability with an
available fix (SEC-VULN-002). On a new platform it fails before any app is
added, for two reasons outside this repository:

- **Operating-system packages.** The Frappe base image is pinned by digest
  (SEC-VULN-005) and Debian publishes security fixes after it was built.
- **Files that come with Frappe.** Frappe's `node_modules` (including build
  tools such as esbuild) and the Python packages it pins in the bench
  environment. They change only with a Frappe release.

## Decision

- The production stage of `docker/Dockerfile` runs `apt-get upgrade`, so every
  build gets the Debian security fixes available at build time.
- Findings in Frappe's own files are accepted in `.trivyignore.yaml`, scoped
  to their paths, with an expiry date. `just accept-upstream-vulns` (after
  `just build-image`) writes the file from a scan of the image: it accepts only
  files under `apps/frappe/` and `env/`, never operating-system packages or the
  platform's own apps. The result is reviewed and merged through a pull
  request like any change.
- Entries expire after 45 days by default. When they expire, the scan fails
  again: bump Frappe in `platform.lock` if a release fixes them, otherwise
  review the list and accept it again.

Rejected: skipping Frappe's directories in the scan (no expiry, hides new
findings) and patching Frappe's lockfiles in the image (untested dependency
versions in production).

## Consequences

- The image scan stays a blocking check, and fails on any finding in our apps
  or in operating-system packages.
- `apt-get upgrade` makes builds of the same commit differ over time; the SBOM
  of each release records what it contains (SEC-VULN-001).
- The bench's Python environment also holds the apps' own dependencies: check
  in the review that an accepted Python package is not one of ours.

## Review

When the entries expire, and with each Frappe major version.
